        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has More Protein per Scoop?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Body Fortress has more protein per scoop, 30 g against about 15 g, but Premier has more protein per gram of powder. Both labels list 30 g per serving; Body Fortress gets there with one 45 g scoop and Premier with about two scoops totalling 41 g. That makes Premier about 73% protein by weight and Body Fortress about 67%.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per scoop is the number that matters if you hate measuring: one scoop of Body Fortress is a full 30 g shake. Per gram of powder is the number that tells you what you are paying for. The extra 4 g in each Body Fortress serving is mostly maltodextrin, cocoa and the extra fat that comes with a concentrate-led blend, which is why it carries 30 more calories for the same protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scaled to the 25 g of protein we use for cost comparisons, Premier comes to about 125 calories and Body Fortress to about 150. Over a daily shake that is roughly 750 calories a month, which matters if you are cutting and barely registers if you are bulking. If you are trying to gain weight, Body Fortress's extra carbohydrate is arguably a small plus.
          </p>

          <p>
            The blend order differs too. Premier lists whey isolate first and Body Fortress lists whey concentrate first, and label rules require ingredients in descending order by weight. Isolate carries less lactose and fat than concentrate. Neither brand discloses the ratio. Body Fortress is the only one of the two to publish a leucine figure, about 3.1 g per scoop per the Vitacost listing; Premier's panel gives none, so we cannot compare them on it.
          </p>
        </div>

