        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Third-Party Tested, Body Fortress or Premier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Body Fortress has the stronger testing record of the two, though neither is certified for sport. Body Fortress tubs carry an NSF Contents Certified claim, and our Body Fortress review found Super Advanced Whey listed in NSF's dietary supplement registry under NSF/ANSI Standard 173. We found no third-party certification of any kind for Premier Protein powder at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI 173 is the contents standard: per NSF's description, it checks that what the label says is in the tub and that the product is not contaminated beyond set limits, and it includes facility audits. It is not the same as <a href='https://www.nsfsport.com/' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>NSF Certified for Sport</a>, which adds lot-by-lot screening for substances banned in sport. We found no Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing for either powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most buyers that makes Body Fortress the better-documented product: an outside body has checked its label claims. Our product database still marks the Body Fortress claim as unverified, because the database entry was built from the Vitacost listing, so if this matters to you, search NSF's registry for your flavor before buying. Target lists Premier as gluten-free and kosher without naming a certifier, which is a claim, not a certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drug-tested athletes should skip both. Neither brand claims sport certification for these powders, and a contents listing does not screen for banned substances. Neither product has published heavy-metal results in our database either, so we cannot compare them on lead or cadmium; that is a gap in the data, not a finding about either tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Has the Cleaner Label?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier has the cleaner label for most people: fewer calories, less sugar, fat and sodium, isolate listed first and no soy. Body Fortress's advantages are additions rather than omissions, namely vitamins C and D3 and zinc. Both use the same two artificial sweeteners, sucralose and acesulfame potassium, so neither wins on sweetener.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ingredient by ingredient, per the labels, Premier lists its whey blend, alkalized cocoa, soluble corn fiber, sunflower lecithin, natural and artificial flavors, the two sweeteners, xanthan gum and salt. Body Fortress lists its whey blend, alkalized cocoa, maltodextrin, lecithin, natural flavor, salt, cellulose gum, ascorbic acid, the two sweeteners, zinc oxide and vitamin D3. Each has one gum, which our scoring flags as an additive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maltodextrin is the main mark against Body Fortress. It is a cheap starch-derived carbohydrate that adds bulk and body, and it helps explain the 7 g of carbohydrate per scoop against Premier's 3 g. It is not harmful in these amounts, but it is filler you pay for by weight. Premier uses soluble corn fiber instead, which gives it 1 g of fiber per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens settle it for some readers. Body Fortress lists milk and soy, because its lecithin is soy-derived; Premier uses sunflower lecithin and lists only milk. If you avoid soy, Premier is the one. One point in Body Fortress's favour: it uses natural flavor only, while Premier lists natural and artificial flavors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers tend to describe Premier as a creamy, milkshake-style powder and Body Fortress as a good-value shake that can turn thick. We have not tasted either for this page, so this section summarises buyer reviews and retailer listings, not our own testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier's flavor names, Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Milkshake, Café Latte, Strawberry Milkshake and Chocolate Peanut Butter per Premier's products page, deliberately mirror its shakes, and buyers who like the cartons often say the powder tastes close to them. Some buyers find it too rich or sweet for a plain water shake. Café Latte adds caffeine, about 95 mg per serving per its Amazon listing, so treat it as a morning tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Body Fortress reviews are split rather than bad, per our Body Fortress review. Chocolate and cookies n creme draw the most praise, vanilla is a neutral smoothie base, and strawberry and chocolate peanut butter are more divisive. The most common complaint is thickness, which fits a 45 g scoop with concentrate, maltodextrin and cellulose gum. Buyers who use 10 to 12 ounces of liquid, or a blender, report fewer problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing logistics differ slightly. Body Fortress is one scoop into a big glass; Premier is two scoops into a normal shaker. Both use lecithin, which helps powder disperse in water. If you are unsure, buy the small tub of either first. The 1.78 lb Body Fortress tub at Walmart and the 24.5 oz Premier tub at Target both cost under $30.
          </p>
        </div>

