          </p>

          <p>
            The shake is a different product from either powder. It is built mainly on milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate, so it is casein-heavy and slower to digest, while both tubs are whey blends. Calories are close: a Premier carton at 160 and a Body Fortress scoop at 180, each with 30 g of protein. Body Fortress is not sold as a bottled shake in anything we track, so there is no like-for-like carton comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            The carton costs more for a reason. You pay for packaging, shelf stability and no shaker to wash. For a drink in the car or at a desk, that convenience is real. For a daily post-workout shake at home, the gap adds up. For 30 g a day, a carton a day costs about $67 a month at that Amazon price, against about $35 for a daily Body Fortress scoop at Walmart's 3.9 lb price and about $53 for a daily Premier serving at Target.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical split is to keep cartons for travel and a powder tub at home. If you like the Premier taste, the Premier powder is the natural home tub; if price comes first and Walmart is your store, Body Fortress is. Our <a href='/best-protein-drink/'>best protein drink</a> guide compares Premier shakes with other cartons.
          </p>
