        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the 60 g on the Body Fortress tub as one scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The front of the Body Fortress tub quotes a two-scoop, 90 g serving. One 45 g scoop gives 30 g of protein and 180 calories. Comparing that 60 g against Premier's 30 g makes Body Fortress look twice as strong when, per scoop of shake you actually drink, the protein is the same.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Amazon prices for Body Fortress</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The same 3.9 lb chocolate tub was $45.28 at Walmart and $81.42 on Amazon in our checks, about $0.97 against $1.74 per 25 g. At the Amazon price Premier is cheaper. Check the seller and work out cost per 25 g before buying Body Fortress online; if it is over about $1.25, look at Premier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the NSF listing as sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Body Fortress's NSF/ANSI 173 listing checks label contents and contamination, not banned substances. It is a genuine step up from no testing, but it is not NSF Certified for Sport. Drug-tested athletes should not rely on it, and Premier has no listing at all, so neither suits a tested athlete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Premier powder is the Premier shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cartons are mostly milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate with a vitamin premix; the powder is a whey isolate and concentrate blend with no premix. Calories and taste are close, but the protein type and the price per gram differ. Decide whether you want a home tub or grab-and-go cartons first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the soy line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Body Fortress lists soy among its allergens because of its lecithin, while Premier uses sunflower lecithin and lists only milk. For anyone with a soy allergy that single line decides the comparison, whatever the price gap. Check the allergen statement on the tub in front of you, since formulas change.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Body Fortress or Premier Protein better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Premier Protein is the better powder for most buyers: 30 g of protein for 150 calories against 180 for Body Fortress, about 73% protein by weight against 67%, no soy, and a Label Score of 60 against 51. Body Fortress is the better deal only at Walmart, where the 3.9 lb tub came to about $0.97 per 25 g against $1.47 for Premier at Target.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is cheaper, Body Fortress or Premier Protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the store. At Walmart, Body Fortress's 3.9 lb tub was about $0.97 per 25 g of protein in mid-November, cheaper than Premier's $1.47 at Target. On Amazon the same chocolate tub was about $1.74 against about $1.69 for Premier, though some Body Fortress flavors were cheaper, and at Vitacost Body Fortress was about $1.95. Prices move, so recalculate before buying.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Body Fortress third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Partly. Our Body Fortress review found Super Advanced Whey listed in NSF's dietary supplement registry under NSF/ANSI 173, which checks label accuracy and contaminants and audits the facility. We found no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing, so it is not screened for banned substances. Premier Protein powder had no certification we could find.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Premier Protein powder taste like the shakes?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers often say it tastes close, and the flavor names, such as Chocolate Milkshake and Vanilla Milkshake, mirror the cartons. The recipes differ, though: the shakes are mostly milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate, while the powder is whey isolate and concentrate. We have not tasted either, so this reflects buyer reviews, not our own testing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which has fewer calories, Body Fortress or Premier?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Premier does. Per the labels at the time of writing, Premier gives 30 g of protein for 150 calories, 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 2 g of fat. Body Fortress gives 30 g for 180 calories, 7 g of carbohydrate, 3 g of sugar and 4 g of fat. Scaled to 25 g of protein, that is about 125 calories against 150.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is a Premier Protein shake cheaper than Body Fortress powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. A 12-pack of Premier's 30 g chocolate shakes was $26.74 on Amazon in November, about $1.86 per 25 g of protein. Body Fortress's 3.9 lb tub at Walmart's price was about $0.97 per 25 g, roughly half. Against Body Fortress on Amazon, at about $1.74, the gap is small, and the carton wins on convenience.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Body Fortress or Premier Protein contain soy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Body Fortress lists soy as an allergen, because its lecithin is soy-derived, per the Vitacost label. Premier uses sunflower lecithin and lists only milk, so it is the soy-free choice of the two. Both contain milk and neither suits a dairy allergy. Check the allergen statement on your tub, since formulas and packaging can change.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
